---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
---
<Base title="How this site is funded" description="Where the money comes from, what it does not buy, and why that matters on a site that publishes cost figures.">
<div class="wrap"><Breadcrumbs trail={[{ label: 'How this site is funded', href: '/affiliate-disclosure/' }]} /></div>
<section class="wrap" style="padding:1.5rem 1.25rem 0">
  <p class="eyebrow">Disclosure</p>
  <h1 style="max-width:22ch">How this site is funded</h1>
  <p style="max-width:46ch;font-size:1.12rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin:1.2rem 0 1.8rem">
    A site that publishes cost figures and software prices has an obvious conflict to manage. The
    honest thing is to state where the money comes from, plainly, rather than bury it in a footer.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
      This site is funded by advertising. It carries no affiliate links and earns no commission from
      any company mentioned on it. No vendor pays for coverage, placement, a rating, or the removal
      of a criticism, because there is no commercial arrangement with any of them to pay through.
    </p>

    <h2>Advertising</h2>
    <p>
      Advertisements are served by third-party networks. Those networks select which advertisements
      appear; we do not, and their presence is not an endorsement. Advertisers have no visibility of
      editorial plans and no input into what is published or how a figure is reported.
    </p>
    <p>
      This creates one incentive worth naming: advertising pays by pageview, so there is a pull toward
      writing more pages rather than better ones. The defense against that is the same as everything
      else here — a page that does not carry a figure worth checking does not get published, and the
      <a href="/corrections/">corrections log</a> records it publicly when one turns out to be wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>Software pricing pages</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/reviews/">software pricing section</a> records what vendors and named sources
      publish, with the date each figure was checked. Those pages are references rather than tested
      reviews, and they say so at the top. No tool listed there has paid for inclusion, and none has
      been given an opportunity to review a page before it went live.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a scored review is ever published, the tool will have been bought at list price under the
      <a href="/how-we-test/">published rubric</a>, and the plan tier and month tested will be stated
      on the page.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this matters more here than on most sites</h2>
    <p>
      A recurring finding across these guides is that most cost estimates in this industry are
      published by firms selling the remedy — reimbursement recovery services quoting how much you are
      losing, freight forwarders quoting freight rates, capital providers quoting the cost of not
      having capital.
    </p>
    <p>
      That criticism applies to this site too, and the defense is not that we are unusually virtuous.
      It is that every figure carries a named source and a retrieval date, so you can check it against
      the primary source yourself. Where published sources disagree, the disagreement is shown rather
      than resolved silently in anyone's favor.
    </p>

    <h2>What we do not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No paid placements, sponsored posts, or paid-for coverage.</li>
      <li>No affiliate or referral commissions from any company mentioned.</li>
      <li>No vendor-supplied review accounts, and no pre-publication review by anyone covered.</li>
      <li>No selling of reader data. See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      If you want to know whether anything on this site was paid for, ask. The
      <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> is the route, and the answer will be a straight yes or no.
    </p>

    <p class="note">
      {SITE.name} is an independent publication. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or sponsored
      by Amazon, Walmart, eBay, Etsy, TikTok, AliExpress, Shopify, or any of the software vendors
      discussed. All trademarks belong to their respective owners.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
